  "slug": "probabilistic-brain-connections-c47e",
  "summary": "Recent preprints target the reliability of brain network estimates and link connectivity changes to E/I balance, microglia and receptor maps.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is anchoring regional brain measures to network-level and molecular organization. GROW introduces a cluster-growing framework for functional coordinate network mapping aimed at improving the robustness of Alzheimer's disease brain network maps. In people at clinical high risk for psychosis, resting-state fMRI Hurst exponent reductions are linked to excitation/inhibition balance and receptor distributions, with preliminary findings. A single-woman longitudinal MRI study maps pregnancy-related cortical gray matter loss onto cognitive and neurotransmitter receptor maps. Public human single-nucleus data show sparse adrenergic receptor detection in microglia and an age-linked shift from homeostatic to inflammatory hippocampal microglia. This thread is not shared by all items: GROW is primarily methodological, and the microglia study rests on sparse detection in public data. What is new is the explicit pairing of imaging or transcriptomic readouts with receptor-level biology.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
